+(durations)=
+
+### Durations
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a string such as `1h30m` to a `datetime.timedelta`. A value is one or more integer
+parts, each followed by a unit: `d`, `h`, `m`, `s`, or `ms`. The units must appear in that order, each at most once, with
+no separators. A bare integer is a number of seconds, so `90` is the same as `90s`. Negative values are rejected.
+
+Like {class}`IntRange`, `min` and `max` can be passed to limit the accepted values. Both bounds are inclusive.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration())
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(timeout)
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1m500ms'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=2d4h'])
+```
+
